  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative interviews from only two metropolitan areas in a single country, which may limit generalizability to older or differently structured metropolitan systems.",
      "what": "This study analyzes political-administrative relations in the newly established metropolitan areas of Czechia using semi-structured interviews with politicians and civil servants from two metropolitan regions. The research reveals that civil servants wield significant influence due to their technical expertise in project management and EU funding, while politicians remain largely disengaged from the metropolitan agenda.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the institutional weakness and uncertain status of emerging metropolitan governance structures stem directly from a political class focused on short-term local electoral benefits rather than regional coordination, leaving bureaucrats to drive the agenda by default."
    }
